package Lessons

use Libraries.Web.Page.all
use Libraries.Web.Page.Standards.ComputerScienceStandards2026
use Libraries.Web.Page.Standards.QuorumStandards

class SceneTerrainLakes is QuorumPage
    // The site's sets of standards, built once for every page.
    QuorumStandards standards

    on create
        SetLocation("tutorials/Scenes/terrainLakes.html")
    end

    action Setup
        // For search engines and link previews.
        SetDescription("Learn to make lakes and puddles on a Quorum Studio terrain with the Fill water and Paint water tools, and the algorithm that fills a hollow.")

        Title("Lakes")

        Overview()
        WarmUp()
        Explore()
        Engage()
        WrapUp()
    end

    action Overview
        Section("Overview")
        Paragraph("A sea covers everything below one height, but lakes, ponds and puddles sit
at their own heights, in the hollows of the land. Quorum Studio has two
tools for them. "):Strong("Fill water"):Text(" pours water into a hollow and lets it rise
the way real water does, until it would spill over the edge. "):Strong("Paint
water"):Text(" lays a thin layer of water exactly where you brush it, for
puddles, marshes and shallow streams. In this tutorial, you will make
lakes with both tools, change a lake's level, and learn the step-by-step
rule, an algorithm, that Quorum Studio follows to fill a hollow. At the
end, a short program checks whether a player is standing in the water.")

        Subsection("Goals")
        Goals()
        ListItem("Fill a hollow with the Fill water tool, and change the lake's level")
        ListItem("Paint and erase shallow water with the Paint water tool")
        ListItem("Describe the algorithm that fills a hollow, and test it with cases where
it should and shouldn't make a lake")
        ListItem("Write a program that asks a terrain whether a point is under water, and
how deep the water is there")
    end

    action WarmUp
        Section("Warm up")
        Paragraph("Pour a glass of water onto a bumpy table in your mind. Where does it go
first? When does it stop spreading? What happens if one side of a dip is
lower than the others? Try to write the rules down in three or four short
steps that anyone could follow.")

        Subsection("Vocabulary")
        Paragraph("You will be learning about the following vocabulary words:")
        Table("Vocabulary", "media/scenes/Terrain/Lakes/Vocabulary.csv")

        Subsection("New Code to Learn")
        Paragraph("You will be using the following new pieces of code:")
        Table("New Code to Learn", "media/scenes/Terrain/Lakes/Code.csv")

        Subsection("CSTA Standards")
        ComputerScienceStandards2026 computerScience = standards:GetComputerScience2026()
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:HIGH_SCHOOL_ALGORITHMIC_PROBLEM_SOLVING_1)
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:HIGH_SCHOOL_ALGORITHMIC_PROBLEM_SOLVING_3)
        StandardsList(computerScience)
    end

    action Explore
        Section("Explore")
        Paragraph("This tutorial needs a terrain with hollows: a few dips made with the Lower
tool in "):Link("Shaping Terrain", "/tutorials/Scenes/terrainShape.html"):Text(", or a
generated landscape from
"):Link("Generating Terrain", "/tutorials/Scenes/terrainGenerate.html"):Text(". It also helps
to make one long slope with no dip in it, for testing. The water's colors
and ripples are the same as the sea's, set in the Water section's
"):Strong("Look"):Text(" part, as "):Link("The Sea", "/tutorials/Scenes/terrainSea.html"):Text(" explains.")
        WaterTools()
        FillingHollow()
        HowFillWaterDecides()
        ChangingLake()
        PaintingWater()
        WaterInGame()
    end

    private action WaterTools
        Subsection("The water tools")
        Table("The Water Tools", "media/scenes/Terrain/Lakes/Keys.csv")
        Paragraph("Both tools are also in the Brush section's Tool group, and in the
"):Strong("Scenes"):Text(" menu ("):Keys("Alt", "C"):Text(", or "):Keys("Option", "C"):Text(" on Mac) under "):Strong("Terrain"):Text(" and then
"):Strong("Tools"):Text(". With either tool chosen, the Brush section shows a "):Strong("Water"):Text("
card with one field, "):Strong("Water depth:"):Text(". It is how far above the ground
under the brush the water's surface goes. It starts at 2.")
        Paragraph("With the mouse, a gray ring follows the pointer. A click outside the
brush's ring moves the brush there, and a click inside it uses the tool.
Holding the button down for a moment uses the tool wherever the pointer
is.")
    end

    private action FillingHollow
        Subsection("Filling a hollow")
        NumberedList()
        ListItem("Choose "):Strong("Fill water"):Text(": press "):Keys("Shift", "Alt", "5"):Text(" ("):Keys("Shift", "Option", "5"):Text(" on Mac).")
        ListItem("Set the "):Strong("Water depth:"):Text(" in the Brush section's Water card. A bigger
number lets the water rise higher.")
        ListItem("Move the brush into a hollow, and press "):Key("Space"):Text(" or click inside the
ring.")
        Paragraph("The water's highest allowed level is the ground's height under the brush
plus the water depth. Quorum Studio then fills the hollow and says what
happened, such as "):DoubleQuote():Output("Filled the hollow: water at 4.3, 812 cells, about 30.0
by 24.0."):DoubleQuote():Text(" The new lake is named "):DoubleQuote():Output("Lake 1"):DoubleQuote():Text(", then "):DoubleQuote():Output("Lake 2"):DoubleQuote():Text(", and so on. Each
fill can be undone.")
        Paragraph("Fill water ignores the brush's size: the water goes wherever it would
really flow, however big the brush is. Pouring again inside a lake that
was filled before fills that lake again from the new spot, and the lake
keeps its name.")
        // Image to add: /media/scenes/Terrain/Lakes/FilledLake.png — A round lake filling a valley between green hills, its shore following the ground.
    end

    private action HowFillWaterDecides
        Subsection("How Fill water decides where water goes")
        Paragraph("Fill water follows an algorithm that copies what real water does. The
ground is treated as a grid of small square "):Term("cells"):Text(", each as wide as the
space between the terrain's height samples. Each cell has one height.")
        NumberedList()
        ListItem():Strong("Run downhill."):Text(" Starting at the brush, the water looks at the four
cells around it, and moves to the lowest one, if it is lower. It repeats
this until no neighbor is lower. Now it is at the bottom of the hollow.
It only runs so far (64 cells), so water poured on a long slope doesn't
end up in a valley far away.")
        ListItem():Strong("Rise, lowest first."):Text(" The water keeps a waiting list of the cells
touching its edge, sorted so the "):Strong("lowest"):Text(" cell always comes out first.
A list like this is called a "):Term("priority queue"):Text(". Each time, the water
covers the lowest waiting cell, and adds that cell's dry neighbors to
the list. Because it always takes the lowest one, it fills the bottom
of the hollow first and spreads upward evenly, as real water does.")
        ListItem():Strong("Stop at the first of three limits:")
        NestedBulletList()
        ListItem():Strong("The level."):Text(" The next lowest cell is at or above the highest level
allowed (the ground under the brush plus the water depth).")
        ListItem():Strong("The rim."):Text(" The next lowest cell is lower than ground the water has
already covered. That means the water has gone over the edge of the
hollow and would run away downhill. The water stops at the rim's
height.")
        ListItem():Strong("The edge of the terrain."):Text(" The water reached a cell with no ground,
so it would run off the world. It stops there too.")
        EndNestedList()
        ListItem():Strong("Wet the low cells."):Text(" Every cell the water reached whose ground is
below the final level gets water. The rest stay dry.")
        Paragraph("When the rim or the edge stopped the water, the report says so, such as
"):DoubleQuote():Output("Filled the hollow: water at 3.1, the hollow's rim, 640 cells, about 26.0
by 20.0."):DoubleQuote():Text(" On a slope with no hollow, the water runs down, then spills over
right away, so no cell is low enough to hold it. Then nothing fills, and
Quorum Studio says "):DoubleQuote():Output("There is no hollow here to hold water: the ground
slopes away, so the water would run off."):DoubleQuote():Text(" Past the edge of the ground, it
says "):DoubleQuote():Output("There is no ground there to pour water on."):DoubleQuote()
        Paragraph("A lake can never float in the air or flood a valley by accident, because
the water only ever stands where ground surrounds it.")
    end

    private action ChangingLake
        Subsection("Changing a lake in the Water section")
        Paragraph("The Water section's "):Strong("Lakes"):Text(" part lists each lake as a part of its own,
which opens with its chevron. Its title says the lake's name, its water
level and about how big it is, such as "):DoubleQuote():Output("Lake 1, level 4.3, about 30 by
24"):DoubleQuote():Text(". Opened, it has two fields and a button:")
        BulletList()
        ListItem():Strong("Name:"):Text(" gives the lake a better name, such as "):Quoted("Mill pond"):Text(".")
        ListItem():Strong("Water level:"):Text(" changes the lake's height. A filled lake is filled
again from its lowest point, so it grows or shrinks to fit the hollow,
and it still stops at the rim. Quorum Studio says, for example, "):DoubleQuote():Output("Mill
pond: water at height 4.8, filling its hollow, about 32.0 by 25.0."):DoubleQuote():Text(" If
the new level is higher than the rim, it says "):DoubleQuote():Output("Mill pond: the hollow
spills over its rim at height 5.2, so the water stops there, about 34.0
by 27.0."):DoubleQuote()
        ListItem():Strong("Remove lake"):Text(" takes the lake away. Quorum Studio says "):DoubleQuote():Output("Removed Mill
pond."):DoubleQuote()
        Paragraph("Each change can be undone, and saving the scene saves the lakes in the
terrain's "):Code("water.json"):Text(" file.")
    end

    private action PaintingWater
        Subsection("Painting water")
        Paragraph("Sometimes you want water that isn't flat: a puddle on a path, a marsh, or a
shallow stream running down a hill. "):Strong("Paint water"):Text(" puts water exactly
where you brush it, standing the water depth above the ground, so it
follows the ground up and down and thins out at its edges.")
        NumberedList()
        ListItem("Choose "):Strong("Paint water"):Text(": press "):Keys("Shift", "Alt", "6"):Text(" ("):Keys("Shift", "Option", "6"):Text(" on Mac).")
        ListItem("Set the brush's "):Strong("Size"):Text(" with "):Keys("Alt", "="):Text(" and "):Keys("Alt", "-"):Text(" ("):Key("Option"):Text(" on Mac), and
a small "):Strong("Water depth:"):Text(", such as 0.3, for a puddle or a stream.")
        ListItem("Hold "):Key("Space"):Text(", or hold the mouse button, and move the brush. Let go to
finish the stroke.")
        Paragraph("If the stroke starts in a lake, it adds water to that lake. Otherwise, it
starts a new lake. When you let go, Quorum Studio says, for example,
"):DoubleQuote():Output("Painted water into Lake 2: 120 cells, water at 6.2."):DoubleQuote():Text(" Painting into a
filled lake only adds water where the ground is below that lake's level,
so the water never floats.")
        Paragraph():Strong("Erasing:"):Text(" hold "):Key("Ctrl"):Text(" as the stroke starts (on a Mac, the "):Key("Control"):Text("
key, then "):Key("Space"):Text(") to erase water from the lake under the brush. Quorum
Studio says "):DoubleQuote():Output("Erased water from Lake 2: 40 cells left."):DoubleQuote():Text(" A lake erased to
nothing is removed: "):DoubleQuote():Output("Erased all of Lake 2, so it was removed."):DoubleQuote():Text(" If there's
no water under the brush, it says "):DoubleQuote():Output("There is no water under the brush to
erase."):DoubleQuote():Text(" Pressing "):Key("Escape"):Text(" while still holding cancels the stroke and puts
the water back, and Quorum Studio says "):DoubleQuote():Output("Stroke canceled"):DoubleQuote():Text(".")
    end

    private action WaterInGame
        // A double quote, for code that has one.
        text quote = ""
        quote = quote:GetDoubleQuote()

        Subsection("Water in a game")
        Paragraph("A game that loads the scene gets its lakes and sea with no extra code. It
can also ask about the water. This program puts a red ball on the ground,
lets the arrow keys roll it around, and once a second writes to Quorum
Studio's output window whether the ball is on dry ground, wading or
swimming. Put it in your project's "):Code("Main.quorum"):Text(", and change the scene's
path and the terrain's name if yours are different.")
        CodeExampleFromFile("sources/tutorials/scenes/SceneTerrainLakes/LakeSwimmer.quorum")
        Paragraph("How it works:")
        BulletList()
        ListItem():Code("FindItem3DByName(" + quote + "Terrain" + quote + ")"):Text(" finds the scene's terrain. It returns an
"):Code("Item3D"):Text(", so the program checks that it really is a "):Code("TerrainItem"):Text(" before
using it as one.")
        ListItem("In "):Code("Update"):Text(", the arrow keys change the ball's x and z, and the ball's
height is set from the ground's height there, so it rolls over hills and
down into the lakes.")
        ListItem():Code("GetWaterDepthAt"):Text(" says how deep the water is above the ground at the
ball's place. It counts the sea and every lake, and is 0 on dry ground.")
        ListItem():Code("IsUnderWater"):Text(" checks one point: here, the middle of the ball, half a
unit above the ground. In water deeper than that, the middle is under
the surface, so the ball is swimming. In shallower water, the ball is
only wading.")
    end

    action Engage
        Section("Engage")
        Paragraph("The goal of this activity is to design a fill algorithm yourselves, and
then to test Quorum Studio's with cases where you know the right answer.
Work with a partner. First, unplugged: draw a grid of 6 by 6 squares on
paper, and write a height from 0 to 9 in each square, so that there is a
hollow in the middle with one low notch in its edge. Choose a square to
pour water on. Together, write the steps you would follow to decide which
squares get water, and at what level it stops. Decide how you will keep
track of the squares at the water's edge that are waiting to be covered,
and which one you take next. Then follow your steps by hand, shading the
wet squares. Compare your steps with the four steps in "):Quoted("How Fill water
decides where water goes"):Text(". Did you use a list sorted lowest first? Did your
water stop at the notch?")
        Paragraph("Next, test the real tool. On a terrain, make four test cases, and before
pouring, write down what you expect Quorum Studio to say for each:")
        BulletList()
        ListItem("a round hollow, poured with a small water depth, so the level stops it;")
        ListItem("the same hollow with a large water depth, so the rim stops it;")
        ListItem("a long slope with no dip at all;")
        ListItem("a hollow that reaches the edge of the terrain.")
        Paragraph("Pour water in each, and record what Quorum Studio actually said or did and how
the lake looks, either visually or using the math behind the lake. Mark each test as passed or failed, and for any that
failed, write what you think went wrong. Finally, try the lake's Water
level field: raise it past the rim and check the result against what you
expected.")
    end

    action WrapUp
        Section("Wrap up")
        Paragraph("Fill water turns a simple idea, "):Quoted("water runs downhill and fills the lowest
place first"):Text(", into steps a computer can follow, with a priority queue to
choose the next cell. Paint water trades that realism for control, for
small water the algorithm wouldn't make. Where else might "):Quoted("always take the
lowest one next"):Text(" be a useful rule? In the next tutorial, "):Link("Rivers", "/tutorials/Scenes/terrainRivers.html"):Text(", you will draw water that
flows along a path from the hills down to a lake or the sea.")
    end
end
